Jonadab the Unsightly One wrote:
> If I understand correctly, you move at the rate of your 
> steed, rather than your own rate.  A horse (that hasn't
> had its speed adjusted) moves at about the same rate
> as a player wearing speed boots, so a typical early-game
> Knight will indeed move faster riding his horse.  I think.
This is mostly correct. You use your own speed if you spent the entire
previous turn performing actions that don't involve the steed (e.g.
picking up items or quaffing potions), and the steed's speed if you
spent any actions on the previous turn moving.

The restrictions are stricter to get gallop speed (from kicking the
steed); at the end of the previous turn, you must have been in the
middle of a travel or farmove (e.g. g-move, shift-move, command-repeated
move). (Thus, if you want to use gallop speed to get a huge number of
actions in a turn, it's best to shift-move at a wall where there's
exactly one free space between you and the wall at the end of the
preceding turn.)

